we're learning new details about the  investigation into last month's deadly  assault on the u.s  capitol the evidence this morning  suggests there were multiple failures to  prepare  and failures to communicate among law  enforcement despite warnings  that an attack was imminent yesterday we  also heard powerful testimony from some  of the police commanders about what they  endured on that terrible day  nicole killian is on capitol hill for us  nicole good morning to you  good morning tony most of the officials  who testified yesterday have since  resigned in the wake of the insurrection  they all  agree this was a well coordinated attack  but one they  didn't see coming these criminals came  prepared for war  in the first hearing into the january  sixth attack former capitol police chief  steven's son said he was  unaware of an fbi bulletin released the  day before  warning violent extremists could descend  on the capitol  you hadn't seen it yourself no ma'am  that raw intelligence came from a  norfolk virginia field office  acting d.c police chief robert congi  says it was sent by email  i would certainly think that something  as violent as an insurrection of the  capitol  would warrant you know a phone call or  something  an fbi spokesperson said the agency  passed on the intelligence within 40  minutes of receiving it  and posted it on a portal available to  law enforcement officers  nationwide cbs news senior national  security analyst fran townsend  says the fbi had an obligation to make  sure the capitol police were aware of  the threat  i think there's a real question not just  their failure to pass the report in the  right way  but why didn't they have it sooner why  didn't they why wasn't that collected  earlier  do you think it could have made a  difference  i absolutely do the capitol hill police  would have had a stronger argument when  they asked for the national guard  sund claimed he was initially denied  national guard troops because optics  were a concern  the former house sergeant of arms  disputed that townsen a former homeland  security adviser says  more resources are needed for capitol  police and that different agencies need  to prepare together  i do think what we've found in a post-9  11 world  is exercises really matter where you  actually go through a scenario with the  interagency so  i think you've got to now look at all  the potential federal targets  and do exercises so everyone understands  what their responsibility is  another hearing will be held next week  with pentagon officials to dive deeper  into some of those national guard delays  and this afternoon  lawmakers will be briefed on the latest  security measures by the acting chief  of capitol police anthony all right  nicole thank you
 

